    aces_size < CIFS_SID_BASE_SIZE)
    break;
ace_size = le16_to_cpu(ace->size);
if (ace_size > aces_size ||
    ace_size < offsetof(struct smb_ace, sid) +
               CIFS_SID_BASE_SIZE)
    break;
```
(and additionally in the lower loop:)
```
if (ace->sid.num_subauth > SID_MAX_SUB_AUTHORITIES)
    break;
```
Reason:
The first new condition requires that the remaining buffer (`aces_size`) is at least as large as the minimal complete ACE structure: header (up to `sid` field) plus the fixed part of a SID (`CIFS_SID_BASE_SIZE`). This ensures that the buffer is large enough to safely read both `access_req` and the base of `sid`. The second new condition now also checks that `ace->size` is at least that same minimum, preventing undersized ACEs that would cause out-of-bounds reads. The additional check in the lower loop (`ace->sid.num_subauth > SID_MAX_SUB_AUTHORITIES`) prevents `compare_sids()` from indexing into the `sub_auth[]` array beyond its declared size, eliminating another out-of-bounds read. Together, these changes close the vulnerability.

